# planegcs, as delivered

This directory is a build of planegcs from FreeCAD @TAG@, the FreeCAD source,
licence and replacement material that goes with it, and the Eigen source and
Boost licence that the library's own build inputs oblige.
`tools/build-planegcs.sh` filled the versions, digests, platform and compiler
in when the build was made, so what you are reading describes this build and
not a general case.

## What is here

| Path | What it is | Licence |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `@LIBRARY@` | the planegcs shared library, built for @PLATFORM@ | LGPL-2.0-or-later |
| `@IMPORT_LIBRARY@` | linker metadata for the library above, on Windows only, where it is what a relink reads. It carries no planegcs implementation | LGPL-2.0-or-later |
| `tree/App/planegcs/` | the complete corresponding source, byte-identical to the release | LGPL-2.0-or-later |
| `tree/boost_graph_adjacency_list.hpp` | from the same release, byte-identical | LGPL-2.0-or-later |
| `sources/eigen-@EIGEN_VERSION@/` | the exact Eigen source this library was compiled against, byte-identical to the checked archive | MPL-2.0, with a few files under more permissive terms; see `COPYING.README` inside it |
| `LICENSE-FreeCAD-LGPL-2.0-or-later.txt` | the full licence text, from the same release | |
| `LICENSE-Eigen-MPL-2.0.txt` | the Mozilla Public License 2.0, copied from `sources/eigen-@EIGEN_VERSION@/COPYING.MPL2` | |
| `LICENSE-Boost-BSL-1.0.txt` | the Boost Software License 1.0, copied from the checked Boost archive | |
| `tree/SketcherGlobal.h`, `tree/FCConfig.h`, `tree/Base/Console.h`, `tree/glue/provenance.cpp` | FerriteCAD's build glue | MIT |
| `PROVENANCE.txt` | the three build inputs, their checked digests and the compiler that built this | MIT |
| `REPLACING.md` | this file | MIT |

**The glue is FerriteCAD's, not FreeCAD's.** Four small files are written by
this project so planegcs builds outside FreeCAD: an export macro, an empty
build-configuration header, a silent console, and the function that returns
the provenance string. They are MIT and say so in their first lines. Nothing
under `tree/App/planegcs/` is touched.

## Provenance

planegcs, LGPL-2.0-or-later:

- source: FreeCAD @TAG@, `src/Mod/Sketcher/App/planegcs`
- archive: `@ARCHIVE_URL@`
- archive SHA-256: `@SHA256@`, verified before anything was extracted

Eigen @EIGEN_VERSION@, MPL-2.0, compiled into the library:

- archive: `@EIGEN_URL@`
- archive SHA-256: `@EIGEN_SHA256@`, verified before anything was extracted
- the source is here, under `sources/eigen-@EIGEN_VERSION@/`, exactly as it came
  out of that archive. Eigen's code ends up inside `@LIBRARY@`, so the Source
  Code Form travels with the binary rather than being a link that may move.

Boost @BOOST_VERSION@, Boost Software License 1.0, headers only:

- archive: `@BOOST_URL@`
- archive SHA-256: `@BOOST_SHA256@`, verified before anything was extracted
- the headers are not here. The licence excepts machine-executable object code
  generated by a source language processor from carrying its notice, and
  `@LIBRARY@` is that. The version, the archive and the digest are recorded
  anyway, because what a build was made from is not a question the licence
  gets to answer, and the licence text is included as a matter of inventory.

This build:

- built on: @PLATFORM@ with @COMPILER@
- the library answers `@PROVENANCE@` when asked what it is

## Replacing it

planegcs is linked dynamically for exactly this reason. Nothing of it is
compiled into a FerriteCAD binary, so you can put your own build in its place
without rebuilding anything of FerriteCAD's.

Rebuild from the sources beside this file. Eigen is one of them; Boost is the
archive named above, and the digest is how you know you have the same one:

```
curl -sSLo boost.tar.gz '@BOOST_URL@'
# sha256sum boost.tar.gz  ->  @BOOST_SHA256@
mkdir boost && tar xzf boost.tar.gz -C boost --strip-components=1

cmake -S <ferritecad>/tools/planegcs -B build \
  -DCMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=Release \
  -DFCAD_PLANEGCS_TREE=$PWD/tree \
  -DFCAD_EIGEN_INCLUDE=$PWD/sources/eigen-@EIGEN_VERSION@ \
  -DFCAD_BOOST_INCLUDE=$PWD/boost \
  -DFCAD_PLANEGCS_PROVENANCE='@PROVENANCE@'
cmake --build build --config Release
```

Or build it any other way you like, from these sources or from a newer
planegcs. What has to hold is the file name and the C++ interface:

- the file must be called `@LIBRARY@` and sit where this one sits;
- it must export the `GCS::` classes declared in `tree/App/planegcs/GCS.h`,
  `Geo.h` and `Constraints.h`, compiled by a toolchain that agrees with
  FerriteCAD's about the C++ ABI and the C runtime;
- on Windows it must also produce `@IMPORT_LIBRARY@`, which is what a relink
  reads;
- it must export `fc_planegcs_provenance()` returning a string that says which
  planegcs it is. `tree/glue/provenance.cpp` is that function; compile it in.
  Nothing loads a library that cannot answer, which is deliberate: it is what
  stops a swapped library from going unnoticed.

Then relink or rerun:

```
FCAD_PLANEGCS_DIR=<this directory> cargo test -p ferritecad-solver-lab \
    --features planegcs -- --nocapture
```

## What this delivery is not

A FerriteCAD release. With its `planegcs` feature enabled,
`ferritecad-viewer` can load this shared library and report its provenance, but
no released FerriteCAD package carries it yet. This directory is the component
delivery used by that build and by the solver gates. Its own native inputs are
complete: the FreeCAD, Eigen and Boost portions are packaged and tested here,
not promised. What a FerriteCAD release still has to add is its own: the
notices for the Rust dependency graph and the SBOM that lists every component,
which `docs/decisions/0002-release-compliance-artifacts.md` records as the work
before the packager.
